    {
      "short": "Hockey stick",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "title": "Why latency explodes near saturation",
      "body": [
        "Queueing theory gives a rule of thumb for one server with random arrivals: <b>wait ≈ work × ρ ÷ (1 − ρ)</b>, where ρ is utilization. From 50% to 60% busy adds a little wait; from 90% to 95% <b>doubles</b> it.",
        "That's the <span class=\"term\">hockey-stick curve</span>: flat for a long stretch, then nearly vertical. The last slice of capacity is by far the most expensive to use."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Don't plan to run at 100%. Plan for the knee of the curve — often 60–75% for latency-sensitive services."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Card shows the formula behind the curve"
      ],
